The directory is empty, so I'll answer generally — if you paste the component and its parent, I can pin down which of these it is.

## The core reason

"Same props" almost always means *structurally equal*, not *referentially equal*. React re-renders a child whenever the parent re-renders, period — it doesn't compare props at all unless you tell it to. And even when you do tell it to (`React.memo`), the comparison is `Object.is` per prop, so a fresh object/array/function identity each render fails it.

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  // NEW identity every render → memo() can't help
  return <Child style={{ margin: 4 }} items={[1, 2]} onClick={() => {}} />;
}
```

## Diagnosing it

1. **Is the child memoized at all?** If `Child` isn't wrapped in `React.memo`, the answer is simply "because the parent re-rendered." That's normal and usually fine.
2. **If it is memoized**, find which prop breaks identity. React DevTools → Profiler → "Record why each component rendered" tells you the exact prop name. Or drop in a quick hook:

```jsx
function useWhyDidYouUpdate(name, props) {
  const prev = useRef(props);
  useEffect(() => {
    const changed = Object.keys({ ...prev.current, ...props })
      .filter(k => prev.current[k] !== props[k])
      .map(k => [k, prev.current[k], props[k]]);
    if (changed.length) console.log(name, changed);
    prev.current = props;
  });
}
```

## The usual culprits

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Inline object/array literal (`style={{...}}`, `items={[...]}`) | Hoist to module scope if constant, else `useMemo` |
| Inline arrow handler | `useCallback` — but only if the child is memoized |
| `children` prop passed as JSX | JSX creates a new element object each render; `memo` on the child won't stop it. Restructure so the parent owning the state doesn't wrap the child |
| Context value `value={{ user, setUser }}` | `useMemo` the value object; every consumer re-renders when the provider's value identity changes, and `memo` does **not** block context updates |
| Selector returning a new object (Redux/Zustand) | Return primitives, or use a shallow-equality selector |
| `useEffect` with an object/array dep | Same identity problem, one layer down |

## Two things worth flagging

**A re-render is not a DOM update.** React re-runs the function and diffs; if the output is identical, nothing touches the DOM. Unless you've profiled an actual problem, chasing re-renders often costs more in code complexity than it saves. Measure first.

**`React.memo` + `useCallback` is a package deal.** `useCallback` alone on a non-memoized child does nothing but add overhead. And if you memoize a child but still pass one unstable prop, you've paid for the comparison and gotten nothing.

The most durable fix is usually structural rather than memoization: move state down so the re-rendering subtree is smaller, or pass the expensive child through `children` from a parent that *doesn't* hold the changing state:

```jsx
// Instead of <Parent> re-rendering <Expensive>:
function App() {
  return <Parent><Expensive /></Parent>;  // element created in App, stable across Parent's renders
}
```

If you're on React 19 with the compiler enabled, most of the manual `useMemo`/`useCallback` work is done for you — worth checking whether it's on before hand-optimizing.
